# Negative test: within the GMN logic glyph plane (denotation context
# gmeow:gmnLogicGlyphContext), a single operator — one lang:denotationTarget — MUST bind
# ONE consistent gmeow:gmnPrecedence within a given result-sort fiber. The logic plane is
# order-sorted: an operator's binding strength is read against operators that produce the
# SAME gmeow:gmnResultSort (Formula operators against Formula operators, Concept
# constructors against Concept constructors, …). If one target has two infix forms in the
# same result-sort fiber declaring DIFFERENT precedences, its binding strength within that
# fiber is undecidable and an operator-precedence parser cannot place it in the ladder.
# Any returned row is that violation.
#
# This is the logic-plane, result-sort-fibered complement of gmn-form-signature-completeness
# (presence) and the math-plane gmn-infix-precedence-consistency (a single flat ladder). It
# deliberately does NOT flag distinct operators sharing a precedence band, nor one operator
# carrying two precedences across DIFFERENT fibers (those never compete in one ladder). The
# only clash it proves from data is one operator clashing with itself inside one fiber. The
# shipped logic plane binds each operator target to one infix form at one precedence, so
# this returns no rows.
PREFIX gmeow: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/>
PREFIX lang: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/lang/>

SELECT ?target ?sort ?formA ?formB ?precA ?precB WHERE {
    ?denA lang:denotationContext gmeow:gmnLogicGlyphContext ;
          lang:denotationTarget ?target ;
          lang:denotedForm ?formA .
    ?denB lang:denotationContext gmeow:gmnLogicGlyphContext ;
          lang:denotationTarget ?target ;
          lang:denotedForm ?formB .
    ?formA gmeow:gmnFixity gmeow:gmnFixityInfix ;
           gmeow:gmnResultSort ?sort ;
           gmeow:gmnPrecedence ?precA .
    ?formB gmeow:gmnFixity gmeow:gmnFixityInfix ;
           gmeow:gmnResultSort ?sort ;
           gmeow:gmnPrecedence ?precB .
    # STR(?formA) < STR(?formB) dedups each distinct-form pair once; ?formA = ?formB adds the
    # self-clash case so ONE form carrying two different gmnPrecedence values in the same fiber
    # is caught too (a normal single-precedence form yields precA = precB and never fires).
    FILTER (STR(?formA) < STR(?formB) || ?formA = ?formB)
    FILTER (?precA != ?precB)
}
ORDER BY ?target ?sort ?formA ?formB
